    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d367">

                <pb facs="0334" n="322" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg322"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.00/1870: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-05-10T11:00:00+03:00">May 10, 1942—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>376. Department’s 203, May 6, 11 p.m. I had intended to discuss this subject last
                    evening with Vyshinski,<anchor n="74" xml:id="d367-f1"/> but in view of Tehran’s
                    145, May 8, 2 [<hi rendition="#italic">1</hi>] p.m., I did not do so.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d367-f1" n="74" xml:id="d367-f1-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, Soviet Deputy People’s Commissar for Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
